From d2418534322514c4cb00e34de1e37368114dc891 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 18:39:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: say at what depth Dolby Vision rebuilt as
 HDR10 leaves

Dolby Vision rebuilt and sent as HDR10 goes out through Kodi's ordinary
HDR10 output: a 10-bit framebuffer, whose link depth i915 and amdgpu
choose. At 3840x2160 above 30 Hz over TMDS, on an HDMI 1.4 TV input, or
with the depths a port has, that is 8 bits per component, and nothing
said so. (NVIDIA has its own way, the half-float output.)

The kernel patches of this series add DVBRIDGE_HDR10_DEEP_COLOR: with
HDR10 metadata on the connector, a commit that would send fewer than 10
bits is refused, with hdr10-depth in DVBRIDGE_REFUSED. Kodi uses it as
a question, never in a commit that is made: once a rebuilt picture has
gone out with its HDR signalling (HDR_OUTPUT_METADATA set, read from the
kernel), a TEST_ONLY commit of the guard alone tells whether the output
in use carries 10 bits or more (CDRMAtomic::TestConnectorProperty()).
The answer goes to the log and to the "Signal" row of the Dolby Vision
page of the player information ("HDR10 at 10 bits or more" or "HDR10 at
8 bits (the link carries no more)"); it is asked again after a change
of mode and with another video. Where the driver has no guard, or does
not answer, nothing is said.

The picture goes out as it did: nothing is changed or refused for it,
also at 8 bits. Falling back to the ordinary path of the video would not
help it: for profile 5 that is a picture in wrong colours, and the
HDR10 base layer of other profiles goes out through the same link at
the same depth.

On top of the Dolby Vision page of "estuary: GPU time, headroom, way of
drawing, frame ahead and step-down on the Dolby Vision page", whose rows
are 44 pixels (seven to a column): the new row is 44 pixels as well. In
the column it goes to, it shows only for a picture rebuilt as HDR10,
where the column has three rows (mode, signal, display); native output,
with its seven, does not show it.
